Z||000000||WaltR||09-10-2001||12:41 PM||||I just finished swapping my 85se V6 from an automatic to an Isuzu 5spd this weekend and have one problem. One of the shift cables is right where the rubber hose from the air cleaner to the throttle body needs to go. I know I am not the only one with this combo. Is there an easy solution using the factory air cleaner? I know I could change to a cone style filter, but did not want to do that now.||||reg||1
Z||000001||jelly2m8||09-10-2001||10:13 PM||||I routed the shift cables underneath the airboot, you will need to turn the airboot a little, so the part towards the front is up a little higher, the cables will be touching the air boot, but so far we haven't encountered any problems.<P> I played with routing the cables over the air boot, but they were interfeering with the throttle.<P> I will get some pics and post them||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||09-10-2001||11:40 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P8060001_1.jpg"> <P>I routed mine underneath the air intake hose.  I believe this setup will work with the stock intake.  Should not affect it being underneath either.   BTW....you are gonna love the Isuzu..I do <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000003||WaltR||09-12-2001||07:40 PM||||Looking at your picture it looks like your cables make a turn towards the driver side after exiting the firewall then go under the throttle body. Mine are routed over the top. That is why I could not put the hose over the cables. Does it not cause any problems with the bend at the firewall or the bend where the cable attaches to the trans? How long have you been running this setup?||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||09-12-2001||10:13 PM||||I believe it was about July when James swapped from a 4cyl auto to V6 5-speed, all in one week (rough!).  We have taken many trips, including all the way from his house to El Paso, IL for the get together there.  As far as I know he hasnt had any shifting problems, and it feels just like my Isuzu in my 4cyl coupe, not as if it is binding or anything.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GarethWright||09-12-2001||10:56 PM||||WaltR, I have been thinking of switching (upgrading) to 5 spd from Auto on my 87 V6, could you please list all that you had to do? Also, where did you get the parts and about how much final cost?  I may do this if I can find a 5-spd Donor car fairly cheap.  Is the Isuzu for the 4cyl the same as the V6?<BR>thanks in advance,<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black86SE||09-12-2001||11:40 PM||||I have also been considering swapping out my auto for a manual. I have an '86 SE V6 but I havent decided whether to go with the 4 speed or the 5. Although I have been told that I shouldn't use the tranny from a 4 cyl. <P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero_1.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000007||WaltR||09-13-2001||03:09 PM||||It is very involved. I took most parts from a 4 cylinder 5spd car. The flywheel is for the V6 but the clutch is for the 4 cylinder. Besides the transmission there is a pedal assembly that you need to hang. This is easier with the dash out. Still difficult, barely fits between dash support and firewall. Shifter and cables need to be installed. This requires disassembly of the console. I was told I would need a steering column but this is BS. Just remove the interlock cable that goes between the shifter and the ignition switch on column. Remove transmission cooler lanes under car and replace with clutch line. I think the stock ecm would work but I changed to a ecm from a manual car. There are also some wiring issues. The auto has one switch on the transmission that controls the park, neutral start, the backup lights and keeps the electric deck lid release from working in gear. The yellow and purple wires are for start and need to be hooked to the clutch pedal switch. My 85 does not have the purple wire going inside the car so I used the yellow one by cutting it about 3 inches from the ignition switch and spliced the wires from the clutch switch into this. The backup light switch just gets the plug spliced on at the transmission. This could be solved with the harness from a stick car but I think changing it would be more work than fixing your harness. One problem is now my deck lid release only works when the clutch is pedal is in. I bought my parts from a pick-your part. It cost me about 500.00 including a new clutch and reman flywheel. A complete 4 Cylinder 5spd car would be easiest to work with.<BR>Parts from wrecking yard.<BR>Transmission (get plug and some wire for backup switches, cable brackets)<BR>Axles (any Fiero manual axles)<BR>Shifter<BR>Shift Cables<BR>Shift console<BR>Pedal assembly (with switches)<BR>Master cylinder<BR>Slave cylinder<BR>Clutch line (get rubber supports by coolant pipe)<BR>The Isuzu 5spd was only used with the 4 cylinder. There are some people on the forum who say it will not live. Without starting a flame war I will just say nobody has come and said "I had one and it did not survive" but there are a number of people using them on V6's and even V8's without problems.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroPerformance||09-13-2001||05:05 PM||||I have on cable going over and one under. Works a lot better than both of them under (I think so anyway). <P>-Charlie||||reg||1
